</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Llama-Mimi: Speech Language Models with Interleaved Semantic and Acoustic Tokens</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Issa Sugiura
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shuhei Kurita
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yusuke Oda
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ryuichiro Higashinaka
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose Llama-Mimi, a speech language model that uses a unified tokenizer and a single Transformer decoder to jointly model sequences of interleaved semantic and acoustic tokens.
Comprehensive evaluation shows that Llama-Mimi achieves state-of-the-art performance in acoustic consistency and possesses the ability to preserve speaker identity.
Our analysis further demonstrates that increasing the number of quantizers improves acoustic fidelity but degrades linguistic performance, highlighting the inherent challenge of maintaining long-term coherence.
We additionally introduce an LLM-as-a-Judge-based evaluation to assess the spoken content quality of generated outputs. Our models, code, and speech samples are publicly available.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        <a href="https://speed1313.github.io/llama-mimi" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://speed1313.github.io/llama-mimi</a></span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
Speech Language Models, Speech Generation, Autoregressive Modeling

</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span> <sup id="id1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♣</span></sup>Kyoto University,
<sup id="id1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♢</span></sup>NII LLMC,
<sup id="id1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‡</span></sup>National Institute of Informatics,
<sup id="id1.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♡</span></sup>Nagoya University</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Speech Language Models (SLMs) approach speech generation as a language modeling task by first converting waveforms into sequences of discrete tokens and then predicting these tokens autoregressively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
Early work established the feasibility of modeling speech with discrete tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, and later advances improved audio quality and long-term consistency through hierarchical tokenization strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These developments demonstrated the potential of speech language models, but often at the cost of increased architectural complexity, such as multi-stage pipelines and specialized tokenizers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
For example, AudioLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> first generates semantic tokens using a single decoder, and then a cascade of models predicts the remaining acoustic tokens conditioned on these semantic tokens.
In contrast, Moshi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> employs an RQ-Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> with a dual-transformer design: a backbone transformer models the temporal sequence across frames, while a depth transformer handles multiple tokens within each frame.
Although effective, such designs complicate training and deployment, requiring careful hyperparameter tuning across multiple models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike these complex pipelines, the natural language processing (NLP) community has demonstrated that large language models with a single Transformer decoder can achieve remarkable performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and have also provided valuable insights such as scaling laws and training stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
These results motivate us to explore a simple, single-decoder approach for speech.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce Llama-Mimi, a speech language model built on a unified tokenizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. By interleaving semantic and acoustic tokens in a single sequence, our model adopts a simple single-decoder Transformer architecture.
We comprehensively assess our models across both acoustic and semantic dimensions using likelihood-based and generation-based methods.
Through extensive evaluation, we demonstrate that Llama-Mimi achieves state-of-the-art performance in acoustic consistency and maintains high speaker similarity.
We also highlight the trade-off between linguistic coherence and acoustic quality.
We further introduce an LLM-as-a-Judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> based evaluation to assess the spoken content quality of generated outputs, providing a more reliable alternative to previously used perplexity-based methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.14882v1/llama-mimi.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/251;" width="476" height="251" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Model architecture of Llama-Mimi.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Speech language models have been rapidly advancing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
GSLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> pioneered speech generation by quantizing self-supervised speech representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> into discrete tokens via k-means and training a Transformer decoder with next-token prediction. However, the acoustic quality remains limited, as these representations are highly correlated with phonetic content but weakly correlated with speaker identity and gender <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. pGSLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> extended this framework by incorporating phonetic and prosodic tokens in a multi-stream Transformer.
AudioLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> improved audio quality and long-term consistency by hierarchically generating semantic and acoustic tokens from a neural audio codec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> in a coarse-to-fine manner with multiple LMs.
TWIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> further demonstrated that initializing a speech model with a pretrained text LM improves performance and speeds up convergence.
MusicGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> studied performance variations by delaying multi-stream token sequences.
More recently, several models have enhanced pre-trained LLMs by incorporating discrete audio tokens, enabling them to handle diverse audio-text tasks such as ASR and TTS with a single Transformer decoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
Spirit-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> interleaved word-level text and speech tokens, supporting joint generation across modalities.
Moshi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> modeled full-duplex dialogue as speech-to-speech generation over multi-stream tokens using an RQ-Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Various generation strategies have been proposed to handle the multi-stream of semantic and acoustic tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
The coarse-to-fine approach first generates the full sequence of semantic tokens, and then produces acoustic tokens conditioned on them. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. While effective, this approach is less suitable for streaming scenarios.
The temporal-plus-depth approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> employs a nested structure: a backbone Transformer models dependencies across frames, while a depth Transformer predicts multiple tokens within each frame. Since the backbone Transformer takes only aggregated frame-level information as input, the model cannot attend to all tokens within each frame, potentially limiting its overall performance.
The interleaved coarse-and-fine approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> aligns semantic and acoustic tokens along the temporal axis and interleaves them at each frame, enabling joint processing of both information within a single modeling. Our method follows this strategy. Spirit-LM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> is an existing example that interleaves multiple tokens, though it relies on different tokenizers for obtaining semantic, pitch, and style tokens. Our work further leverages unified tokenization strategy as well as interleaved generation, allowing us to manage only a single model stack for the entire processing.</p>
</div>
