<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Llama-Mimi</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Llama-Mimi: Speech Language Models with Interleaved Semantic and Acoustic Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides an overview of our approach.
We build upon Mimi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, a neural audio codec that converts audio waveforms into residual vector quantizers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Mimi follows the unified tokenizer approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, in which the first-level quantizers are semantically distilled from WavLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
Given a single-channel audio waveform <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first represent <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as a sequence of discrete tokens <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}</annotation></semantics></math> using Mimi:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}=(y^{1}_{1},y^{2}_{1},\dots,y^{Q}_{1},y^{1}_{2},\dots,y^{Q}_{T^{\prime}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}=(y^{1}_{1},y^{2}_{1},\dots,y^{Q}_{1},y^{1}_{2},\dots,y^{Q}_{T^{\prime}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of quantizers, <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}\ll T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≪</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}\ll T</annotation></semantics></math>) the number of frames, and <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{q}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{q}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> the token from the <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-th quantizer at time <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Tokens within each frame are ordered from the first to the <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>-th quantizer.
We model this sequence directly with a Transformer decoder.
We use Llama 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> as our backbone Transformer decoder.
By predicting semantic tokens first and conditioning subsequent acoustic tokens on them within each frame, the model is expected to generate linguistically coherent speech with fine-grained acoustic detail.
To enable Llama 3 to process audio tokens, we extend its vocabulary with audio tokens and special tokens <span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;audio&gt;</span> and <span id="S3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/audio&gt;</span>. Training then follows the standard next-token prediction objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
At inference, the model autoregressively generates tokens as in conventional LLMs. The generated tokens are sequentially decoded into audio frames by Mimi, and the process terminates once the <span id="S3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/audio&gt;</span> token is produced. During sampling, tokens are selected from the combined text and audio vocabulary. While this doesn’t strictly enforce Mimi’s RVQ order (<math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\rightarrow 2\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\rightarrow 2\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow Q</annotation></semantics></math>), empirically the model naturally follows this sequence, enabling generation without token constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ the 1.3B and 8B variants of Llama 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> as our backbone Transformer decoder architectures, hereafter referring to them as Llama-Mimi-1.3B and Llama-Mimi-8B.
To balance sequence length and reconstruction quality, we use <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=4</annotation></semantics></math> codebooks at a frame rate of 12.5Hz, yielding 50 tokens per second.
Following  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, we initialize our models with pretrained Llama 3 checkpoints while keeping Mimi’s parameters frozen.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train our models on publicly available audio corpora, including Libri-Light <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, The People’s Speech <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, VoxPopuli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and Emilia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
Restricting to the English subsets, the combined training data amounts to approximately 240k hours of audio.
All samples are truncated to a maximum of 20 seconds.
We have confirmed that the truncated dataset preserves a broad duration distribution, spanning from 1 to 20 seconds, which enables the model to learn to generate audio of diverse lengths.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hyperparameters.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train our models with the AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
The global batch size is set to 1,024, and the maximum sequence length is set to 1,024 tokens.
The learning rate follows the Warmup-Stable-Decay (WSD) schedule <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> over 100k steps: it warms up to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> in 1.5k steps, stays constant for 80% of training, and linearly decays to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> in the final 20%.
Our models are trained on 32 NVIDIA H200 GPUs with FSDP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and training Llama-Mimi-1.3B takes approximately 48 hours.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our models against several baselines using both likelihood-based and generation-based metrics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use several representative baselines. The SSL-based GSLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and TWIST-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> perform strongly on semantic tasks. Flow-SLM-1B-ext <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, which is based on conditional flow matching (CFM), excels at acoustic tasks. Moshi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> uses Mimi as the audio tokenizer and follows a temporal-plus-depth approach.
Since Moshi’s checkpoint after the audio-only pretraining stage has not been publicly released, we report its scores from  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
