</section>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Likelihood-based evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate both acoustic and semantic knowledge using likelihood-based benchmarks.
For acoustic tasks, we use SALMon<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, which is divided into two categories: acoustic consistency and acoustic-semantic alignment. Acoustic consistency task evaluates whether the model assigns higher likelihood to a natural sample compared with one where an acoustic property (e.g., speaker identity or sentiment) changes unnaturally within an utterance. Acoustic-semantic alignment task evaluates whether the model assigns higher scores to samples where acoustic signals match semantic factors (e.g., sentiment, background sounds), compared with mismatched and thus unnatural ones.
For semantic tasks, we use sWUGGY <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, sBLIMP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, and sTopic-StoryCloze<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
sWUGGY evaluates whether the model assigns higher probability to real words over pseudo-words (e.g., “oscillation” vs. “odenacia”), while sBLIMP evaluates whether the model prefers grammatically correct sentences (e.g., “The sweater isn’t folding” vs. “The sweaters isn’t folding”).
sTopic-StoryCloze measures continuation coherence given a spoken prompt.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, which computes perplexity only over semantic tokens, we restrict the perplexity calculation in Llama-Mimi to semantic tokens for all tasks except SALMon. In our experiments, we found that excluding acoustic tokens leads to better performance on all benchmarks except SALMon.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Llama-Mimi: Speech Language Models with Interleaved Semantic and Acoustic Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the results.
Our models achieve the best performance on the SALMon acoustic consistency task.
We attribute their strong acoustic capability to our modeling approach, which enables the model to attend to all past tokens and capture fine-grained representations of subtle acoustic variations.
For semantic tasks, our models perform slightly worse than the SSL-based TWIST model, likely because the longer sequence lengths hinder effective capture of global information.
We also find that model size has minimal effect on acoustic tasks but improves the performance on semantic tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
