</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Generation-based evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our models in a speech generation setting, where a short audio prompt is given and the model produces a continuation. The generated audio is then assessed for speaker consistency and spoken content quality. We use the LibriSpeech test-clean set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> (2,620 examples), truncating each sample to the first three seconds as the prompt and generating continuations of up to 20 seconds. The sampling parameters are set to temperature 0.8 and top-k 30.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Speaker consistency.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To measure whether the continuation preserves the speaker identity, we extract embeddings with pyannote’s WavLM-based model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and compute cosine similarity between the prompt and continuation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Spoken content quality.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> propose evaluating spoken content quality by computing perplexity on transcriptions of generated speech. However, perplexity is unstable, as it is highly sensitive to sequence length and sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. To address this, we adopt an LLM-as-a-Judge approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We first transcribe both the prompt and continuation with Whisper Turbo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, then ask GPT-4o (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4o-2024-11-20</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> to rate the continuation from 1 (worst) to 10 (best). The temperature is set to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure deterministic scoring. The evaluation prompt is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="113.3" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 113.3" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,113.3) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A6A6A6;" fill="#A6A6A6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 9.26 L 0 104.04 C 0 109.15 4.14 113.3 9.26 113.3 L 468.12 113.3 C 473.23 113.3 477.38 109.15 477.38 104.04 L 477.38 9.26 C 477.38 4.14 473.23 0 468.12 0 L 9.26 0 C 4.14 0 0 4.14 0 9.26 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 9.26 L 1.38 104.04 C 1.38 108.39 4.91 111.92 9.26 111.92 L 468.12 111.92 C 472.47 111.92 475.99 108.39 475.99 104.04 L 475.99 9.26 C 475.99 4.91 472.47 1.38 468.12 1.38 L 9.26 1.38 C 4.91 1.38 1.38 4.91 1.38 9.26 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.19 12.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:35.23em;--ltx-fo-height:6.63em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.23em;font-size:10pt;" height="94.78" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 91.67)" width="487.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:35.23em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Given the following prompt and completion, rate the quality of the completion on a scale from 1 to 10, where 10 is the best possible completion. Consider relevance, coherence, fluency, and informativeness. Output only the score as an integer.
<br class="ltx_break">Prompt: {prefix} 
<br class="ltx_break">Completion: {suffix}</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison with LLM-as-a-Judge based evaluation, we also report perplexity using GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> on transcriptions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare against TWIST-1.3B and GSLM. Flow-SLM-1B-ext and Moshi are excluded, as audio-only pretrained versions are not available.
Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Results. ‣ 5.1 Likelihood-based evaluation ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Llama-Mimi: Speech Language Models with Interleaved Semantic and Acoustic Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the results.
For speaker similarity, our models achieve higher scores than baselines, demonstrating stronger preservation of speaker identity.
For spoken content quality, as expected, perplexity is unreliable: shorter continuations are penalized, and even the ground-truth reference receives the worst score.
In contrast, the LLM-as-a-Judge scores are more consistent: larger models achieve higher scores, and the ground-truth reference obtains the best score, indicating that the evaluation is reasonable.
Under the LLM-as-a-Judge evaluation, Llama-Mimi-1.3B attains scores between GSLM-151M and TWIST-1.3B, consistent with the likelihood-based ranking.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Qualitative generation examples</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We qualitatively assess the outputs of our models using audio prompts from the TWIST demo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Baselines. ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Llama-Mimi: Speech Language Models with Interleaved Semantic and Acoustic Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Llama-Mimi-8B produces more natural continuations than Llama-Mimi-1.3B, indicating that larger models are more effective at handling long sequences and preserving coherence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
