</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Effect of quantizer number</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the effect of the quantizer number against audio quality, speaker similarity, and spoken content quality.
We evaluate audio quality using Audiobox-Aesthetics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, which scores audio along four dimensions: Production Quality (PQ), Production Complexity (PC), Content Enjoyment (CE), and Content Usefulness (CU).
As in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Generation-based evaluation ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Llama-Mimi: Speech Language Models with Interleaved Semantic and Acoustic Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we use LibriSpeech test-clean and employ the 1.3B model while varying the number of quantizers (<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in\{2,4,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in\{2,4,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 Qualitative generation examples ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Llama-Mimi: Speech Language Models with Interleaved Semantic and Acoustic Tokens" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that increasing the number of quantizers improves both audio quality and speaker similarity, but decreases spoken content quality.
This result aligns with the findings of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced Llama-Mimi, a speech language model that uses a unified tokenizer and a single Transformer decoder. Our model achieved state-of-the-art performance in acoustic consistency and possesses the ability to preserve speaker identity.
We also revealed a trade-off between audio fidelity and long-term coherence. We also introduced an LLM-as-a-Judge-based spoken content quality evaluation framework that provides more reliable and realistic assessments than perplexity-based metrics.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
